"Microsoft Word - LLN 2006-006 Frontpage.doc (https://www.parliament.uk/globalassets/documents/lords-library/hllsalisburydoctrine.pdf)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 87; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view1\",\"id\":85}); Total lines: 3257\nL0@P0: The Salisbury Doctrine\nL1@P0: (Updated June 2006)\nL2@P0: Glenn Dymond and Hugo Deadman\nL3@P0: 30th June 2006\nL4@P0: LLN 2006/006\nL5@P0-1: HOUSE OF LORDS LIBRARY LIBRARY NOTEHouse of Lords Library Notes are compiled for the benefit of Members of Parliament and\nL6@P1: their personal staff. Authors are available to discuss the contents of the Notes with the\nL7@P1: Members and their staff but cannot advise members of the general public.\nL8@P1: Any comments on Library Notes should be sent to the Head of Research Services, House of\nL9@P1-2: Lords Library, London SW1A 0PW or emailed to victoryi@parliament.uk.Contents\nL10@P2:  Introduction p. 1\nL11@P2: 1. The mandate doctrine p. 2\nL12@P2:  (i) The Disestablishment of the Irish Church p. 3\nL13@P2:  (ii) The Municipal Elections Bill 1872 p. 6\nL14@P2:  (iii) The development of the doctrine p. 7\nL15@P2:  (iv) The Representation of the People Bill 1884 p. 10\nL16@P2:  (v) The Government of Ireland Bill 1893 p. 12\nL17@P2:  (vi) The Liberal Government 1892–95 p. 13\nL18@P2:  (vii) The Liberal Governments from 1905 p. 14\nL19@P2:  (viii) The Parliament Bills 1910–11 p. 16\nL20@P2: 2. The Labour Government 1945–51 p. 21\nL21@P2:  (i) The Salisbury doctrine 1945–51 p. 21\nL22@P2:  (ii) The Parliament Bill 1948–49 p. 23\nL23@P2:  (iii) The Iron and Steel Bill 1948–49 p. 27\nL24@P2: 3. The Labour Governments of 1964–70 and 1974–79 p. 31\nL25@P2:  (i) The Aircraft and Shipbuilding Bill 1975–77 p. 32\nL26@P2:  (ii) The Scotland Bill 1978 p. 33\nL27@P2: 4. The Conservative Government of 1979–90 p. 36\nL28@P2:  (i) The Local Government (Interim Provisions) Bill 1984 p. 36\nL29@P2:  (ii) The Local Government Bill 1985 p. 39\nL30@P2:  (iii) The Local Government Finance Bill 1988 p.42\nL31@P2: 5. The Current Debate p. 45\nL32@P2:  (i) The debate on the Salisbury doctrine in the House of Lords, p. 45\nL33@P2-3:  19th May 1993  (ii) The Politeia Lecture by Lord Cranborne, 4th December 1996 p. 48\nL34@P3: \nL35@P3:  (iii) The Mackay Commission Report, 16th April 1999 p. 52\nL36@P3:  (iv) The Politeia Lecture by Lord Strathclyde, 30th November 1999 p. 53\nL37@P3:  (v) The Wakeham Commission Report, January 2000 p. 55\nL38@P3:  (vi) The debate on the Parliament Acts and the Salisbury p. 57\nL39@P3:  Convention in the House of Lords, 24th January 2001\nL40@P3:  (vii) The Government response to the Wakeham Commission, p. 60\nL41@P3:  November 2001\nL42@P3: \nL43@P3:  (viii) The Joint Committee on House of Lords Reform, p. 61\nL44@P3:  First and Second Reports, December 2002 and April 2003\nL45@P3:  (ix) The DCA Consultation Paper, ‘Next steps for the p. 61\nL46@P3:  House of Lords’, September 2003\nL47@P3:  (x) The Labour Peers Working Group on House of Lords Reform, p. 62\nL48@P3:  20th July 2004\nL49@P3: \nL50@P3:  (xi) The Politeia pamphlet ‘Working in Harness’ by Lord p. 64\nL51@P3:  Strathclyde, April 2005\nL52@P3: \nL53@P3: (xii) The DCA Paper, ‘Making a Difference’, May 2005 p. 65\nL54@P3: (xiii) The debate on the humble Address in reply to the Queen’s p. 65\nL55@P3:  Speech in the House of Lords, May 2005\nL56@P3: (xiv) The debate on Parliament and the Legislative Process in the p. 67\nL57@P3:  House of Lords, 6th June 2005\nL58@P3: (xv) The Joint Committee on Conventions , May 2006 p. 68\nL59@P3-4: 6. References p. 80 1\nL60@P4: Introduction\nL61@P4: The Salisbury doctrine, as generally understood today, means that the House of Lords should\nL62@P4: not reject at second or third reading Government Bills brought from the House of Commons\nL63@P4: for which the Government has a mandate from the nation. It had its origins in the doctrine of\nL64@P4: the mandate developed by the third Marquess of Salisbury, Prime Minister in 1885 and from\nL65@P4: 1886–1892 and 1895–1902, as part of his effort to perpetuate the influence of the House in an\nL66@P4: age of widening suffrage. Salisbury, a Conservative who sat in the Lords from 1868 until his\nL67@P4: death in 1903, developed a doctrine of the mandate over this period which argued that the\nL68@P4: will of the people and the views expressed by the House of Commons did not necessarily\nL69@P4: coincide, and that in consequence, the House of Lords had an obligation to reject, and hence\nL70@P4: refer back to the electorate, particularly contentious Bills, usually involving a revision of the\nL71@P4: constitutional settlement, which had been passed by the Commons.\nL72@P4: Since 1945, the Salisbury doctrine has been taken to apply to Bills passed by the Commons\nL73@P4: which the party forming the Government has foreshadowed in its General Election manifesto,\nL74@P4: being particularly associated with an understanding between Viscount Addison, the Leader of\nL75@P4: the House of Lords, and Viscount Cranborne (the fifth Marquess of Salisbury from 1947),\nL76@P4: Leader of the Opposition in the Lords, during the Labour Government of 1945–51; and thus\nL77@P4: is sometimes called the Salisbury/Addison doctrine. Lord Carrington later described the\nL78@P4: convention as extending to any wrecking amendment to a manifesto measure (Lord\nL79@P4: Carrington, Reflect on Things Past: The Memoirs of Lord Carrington (1988), pages 77–78).\nL80@P4: This Lords Library Note describes the origins of the Salisbury doctrine in the 1860s, its\nL81@P4: development until the passing of the 1911 Parliament Act formalised certain aspects of the\nL82@P4: legislative relationship between the House of Lords and the House of Commons, and its\nL83@P4: subsequent application after 1945. The first section sets out the position taken by the Duke of\nL84@P4: Wellington as Leader of the House of Lords at the time of the Reform Act of 1832, traces the\nL85@P4: development of the mandate doctrine by the third Marquess of Salisbury, and rehearses the\nL86@P4: arguments used for and against its subsequent application during the debates on a number of\nL87@P4: controversial Bills in the House of Lords up until 1911. The second section addresses the\nL88@P4: restatement of the doctrine from 1945 to 1951, and its application during the passage of the\nL89@P4: Parliament Bill and the Iron and Steel Bill through the House of Lords. The third section\nL90@P4: refers briefly to the relationship between the House of Lords and the Labour Governments of\nL91@P4: 1964–70 and 1974–79, and in particular traces the passage of the Aircraft and Shipbuilding\nL92@P4: Industries Bill 1975–77 and the Scotland Bill 1978. The fourth section looks at the\nL93@P4: application of the Salisbury doctrine under the Conservative Government of 1979–90, in\nL94@P4: particular tracing the passage of the Local Government (Interim Provisions) Bill 1984, the\nL95@P4: Local Government Bill 1985 and the Local Government Finance Bill 1988. The fifth section\nL96@P4: of the Note outlines the development of the debate on the Salisbury doctrine since the 1990s.\nL97@P4: The Note touches upon a number of issues relating to the debate over reform of the House of\nL98@P4: Lords. However, it does so only in as much as the Salisbury doctrine applies to that debate.\nL99@P4: The Note does not attempt to set out the arguments for and against reform of the composition\nL100@P4: and powers of the House.\nL101@P4: The Note ends with a set of references to parliamentary, biographical and other sources.\nL102@P4: The Note updates the previous Lords Library Notes on The Salisbury Doctrine of 19th March\nL103@P4-5: 1997 (LLN 97/004) and 14th June 2005 (LLN 2005/004)2\nL104@P5: 1. The mandate doctrine\nL105@P5: In his book on the Victorian constitution, Godfrey Le May writes of the House of Lords and\nL106@P5: its leadership by the Duke of Wellington during the passage of the Reform Act 1832, as\nL107@P5: follows:\nL108@P5: Wellington’s consistent conviction was that it was better to consent to a bad Bill than\nL109@P5: to run a serious risk of civil disturbance. This was the principle upon which he urged\nL110@P5: the Lords to pass Catholic Emancipation, Reform and the repeal of the Corn Laws.\nL111@P5: On the last of these measures, he is supposed to have told a protectionist peer who\nL112@P5: expressed a bad opinion of it: ‘Bad opinion of the Bill, my lord! You can’t have a\nL113@P5: worse opinion of it than I have, but it was recommended from the throne; it was\nL114@P5: passed by the Commons by a large majority, and we must all vote for it. The Queen’s\nL115@P5: Government must be supported’... The greater wisdom was to pass [the Reform Act]\nL116@P5: at once, although this might seem to be against their immediate interests. This might,\nL117@P5: on a narrow view, seem tantamount to saying that the Lords could only keep their\nL118@P5: powers if they did not use them; but the argument could be developed beyond the\nL119@P5: trivial point. The House of Lords, it ran, was the ultimate guardian of the\nL120@P5: constitution. It must therefore preserve its powers intact against the day when it\nL121@P5: might have to spend them upon some great occasion.\nL122@P5: (G. H. L. Le May, The Victorian Constitution: Conventions, Usages and\nL123@P5: Contingencies (1979), pp. 129–30)\nL124@P5: Walter Bagehot summarised the subordinate position of the House of Lords in the middle of\nL125@P5: the nineteenth century as follows:\nL126@P5: Since the Reform Act the House of Lords has become a revising and suspending\nL127@P5: House. It can alter Bills; it can reject Bills on which the House of Commons is not\nL128@P5: yet thoroughly in earnest – upon which the nation is not yet determined. Their veto is\nL129@P5: a sort of hypothetical veto. They say, We reject your Bill for this once or these twice,\nL130@P5: or even these thrice: but if you keep on sending it up, at least we won’t reject it. The\nL131@P5: House has ceased to be one of latent directors, and has become one of temporary and\nL132@P5: palpable alterers.\nL133@P5: (Walter Bagehot, The English Constitution (1958 edition, p. 88))\nL134@P5: Corinne Comstock Weston, Professor of History at the City University of New York, notes\nL135@P5: that:\nL136@P5: Both Wellington and Bagehot assumed that the House of Lords must take its direction\nL137@P5: from the House of Commons as the nation’s spokesman.\nL138@P5: But she argues that the third Marquess of Salisbury proposed a very different view of the role\nL139@P5: of the Lords, according to which:\nL140@P5: the House of Lords had a referendal function: it had the duty of referring measures to\nL141@P5: the electorate or nation whenever important questions arose and there was ground for\nL142@P5-6: believing that the Government, resting on the House of Commons, lacked a mandate 3\nL143@P6: for its measures. Only if a mandate was forthcoming would or should the House of\nL144@P6: Lords permit a disputed measure on a vital question to pass into law. The most\nL145@P6: striking aspect of the referendal theory was the insistence that the political barometer\nL146@P6: for the peers to watch was not the House of Commons – so central to Wellington and\nL147@P6: Bagehot – but the nation itself. To the nation the House of Lords should look for\nL148@P6: guidance and direction; and only to the nation’s will, as registered at the polls, would\nL149@P6: that House bow. But that it would bow to the nation’s will was stated over and over\nL150@P6: again.\nL151@P6: (Corinne Comstock Weston, ‘Salisbury and the Lords, 1868–1895’, Peers, Politics\nL152@P6: and Power: The House of Lords, 1603–1911, eds. Clyve Jones and David Lewis\nL153@P6: Jones (1986), pp. 463–464)\nL154@P6: This section of the Note traces the development of the mandate doctrine by Lord Salisbury\nL155@P6: from 1868 onwards through an analysis of his statements on various contentious Bills which\nL156@P6: were brought to the House of Lords after having been passed by the House of Commons as a\nL157@P6: result of a Liberal majority in that House.\nL158@P6: (i) The Disestablishment of the Irish Church\nL159@P6: In early 1868 Disraeli succeeded Lord Derby as Leader of the Conservative party and became\nL160@P6: Prime Minister of a Government which was in a minority in the House of Commons;\nL161@P6: Gladstone succeeded Lord John Russell as Leader of the Liberal Party.\nL162@P6: On 30th March 1868 Gladstone moved that the Commons resolve itself into a committee to\nL163@P6: consider the Acts relating to the status of the Established Church of Ireland. Lord Stanley,\nL164@P6: the Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, moved an amendment stating that ‘any proposition\nL165@P6: tending to the disestablishment or disendowment of that Church ought to be reserved for the\nL166@P6: decision of the new Parliament’. In supporting Lord Stanley’s amendment Disraeli said that\nL167@P6: the House of Commons:\nL168@P6: was not morally competent to decide such a question, if those who have elected it had\nL169@P6: not, in the constitutional course of our public life, received some intimation that such\nL170@P6: a question was to come before it ... had the country the slightest intimation during the\nL171@P6: last few years [that this question] would be brought under discussion in Parliament? I\nL172@P6: appeal to the programme of the Prime Minister [Palmerston] of the time, which\nL173@P6: recommended a dissolution of Parliament and explained his policy to the country.\nL174@P6: There is not the slightest allusion to the state of the Irish Church ... We expressed in\nL175@P6: the amendment the opinion that ... it will be inexpedient for the House to enter into\nL176@P6: consideration of the Church in Ireland; and, at the same time we expressed our\nL177@P6: opinion that the decision upon these great points should be reserved for the new\nL178@P6: Parliament.\nL179@P6: (Parliamentary Debates, Third Series, 3rd April 1868, vol. 193, cols. 897–899)\nL180@P6: However, Gladstone’s motion, and a series of resolutions supporting disestablishment were\nL181@P6: subsequently passed by the Commons. As a result Gladstone, Sir George Grey and Mr.\nL182@P6-7: Lawson sponsored the Established Church (Ireland) Bill, which sought to prevent for a 4\nL183@P7: limited time new appointments in the Irish Church and to restrain for a period of time the\nL184@P7: proceedings of the Ecclesiastical Commissioners for Ireland. This Bill passed the Commons\nL185@P7: on 16th June 1868.\nL186@P7: During the second reading debate on the Established Church (Ireland) Bill in the House of\nL187@P7: Lords, a number of Members applied the arguments set out by Disraeli to the particular\nL188@P7: constitutional role of the House of Lords.\nL189@P7: The Lord Chancellor, Lord Cairns, explicitly argued that Gladstone’s Bill lacked a mandate\nL190@P7: from the electorate:\nL191@P7: My Lords, these are the vast issues involved in this Bill. These are the issues\nL192@P7: involved in your Lordships’ decision now, and they are the issues yet to be presented\nL193@P7: to the country in the great appeal to its enlarged constituencies ... In that appeal ... the\nL194@P7: Government will stand as the defenders of all that this Bill and the policy of its\nL195@P7: promoters would seek to overthrow.\nL196@P7: (Parliamentary Debates, Third Series, 26th June 1868, vol. 193, col. 288)\nL197@P7: The Duke of Marlborough said that:\nL198@P7: I have yet to learn that your Lordships’ House does not represent the mind and will of\nL199@P7: the people of England ... By rejecting this Bill your Lordships will be giving an\nL200@P7: opportunity which I think should be given to the people of England, of quietly and\nL201@P7: calmly considering this question.\nL202@P7: (ibid., col. 36)\nL203@P7: Lord Salisbury asked whether the Earl of Clarendon had:\nL204@P7: considered for what purpose this House exists, and whether he would be willing to go\nL205@P7: through the humiliation of being a mere echo and supple tool of the other House in\nL206@P7: order to secure for himself the luxury of mock legislation? I quite admit – everyone\nL207@P7: must admit – that when the opinion of your countrymen has declared itself, and you\nL208@P7: see that their convictions – their firm, deliberate, sustained convictions – are in favour\nL209@P7: of any course, I do not for a moment deny that it is your duty to yield. But there is an\nL210@P7: enormous step between that and being the mere echo of the House of Commons.\nL211@P7:  (ibid., col. 89)\nL212@P7: On 29th June 1868 the House of Lords declined to give a second reading to the Established\nL213@P7: Church (Ireland) Bill by 192 votes to 97.\nL214@P7: At the subsequent General Election, fought largely on the issue of disestablishment of the\nL215@P7: Irish Church, the Liberal Party was returned with a Commons majority of 112. Gladstone’s\nL216@P7: new administration introduced the Irish Church Bill, which was passed by the Commons on\nL217@P7: 31st May 1869. This Bill provided for the winding up of the Irish Ecclesiastical Commission\nL218@P7: and the constitution of a new Commission, appointed for ten years, in which the property of\nL219@P7-8: the Irish Church, subject to life interests, would be vested from the time of the passing of the 5\nL220@P8: Bill. The motion to give the Irish Church Bill a second reading was moved in the House of\nL221@P8: Lords on 14th June 1869. Weston sets out the dilemma facing those members of the House\nL222@P8: who opposed it:\nL223@P8: Should the Irish Church Bill fail the second reading in the House of Lords, after the\nL224@P8: profuse professions of its Members that they would respect the results of the General\nL225@P8: Election, they must wreck the emergent referendal theory at the outset. Conversely,\nL226@P8: reading the Bill a second time, as was done on 19th June, must constitute a milestone\nL227@P8: of the Victorian House of Lords. Such self-restraint under the most trying\nL228@P8: circumstances could be treated as convincing evidence for the proposition that the\nL229@P8: House of Lords would not defy the nation’s will, after that will had been expressed at\nL230@P8: the polls, regardless of the manner in which that House dealt with the House of\nL231@P8: Commons.\nL232@P8: (Corinne Comstock Weston, ‘Salisbury and the Lords, 1868–1895’, Peers, Politics\nL233@P8: and Power: The House of Lords, 1603–1911, eds. Clyve Jones and David Lewis\nL234@P8: Jones (1986), p. 469)\nL235@P8: Lord Salisbury played a key role in sustaining the Irish Church Bill in the face of\nL236@P8: considerable opposition from the Conservative benches. In his speech on the Bill’s second\nL237@P8: reading on 17th June 1869 he said that:\nL238@P8: There may be occasions in our history in which the decision of the House of\nL239@P8: Commons and the decision of the nation must be taken as practically the same. In\nL240@P8: ninety-nine cases out of 100 the House of Commons is theoretically the representative\nL241@P8: of the nation, but it is only so in theory. The constitutional theory has no\nL242@P8: corresponding basis in fact; because in ninety-nine cases out of 100 the nation, as a\nL243@P8: whole, takes no interests in our politics, but amuses itself and pursues its usual\nL244@P8: avocations, allowing the political storm to rage without taking any interest in it. In all\nL245@P8: these cases I make no distinction – absolutely none – between the prerogative of the\nL246@P8: House of Commons and the House of Lords. Again, there is a class of cases small in\nL247@P8: number, and varying in kind, in which the nation must be called into council and must\nL248@P8: decide the policy of the Government. It may be that the House of Commons in\nL249@P8: determining the opinion of the nation is wrong; and if there are grounds for\nL250@P8: entertaining that belief, it is always open to this House, and indeed it is the duty of\nL251@P8: this House to insist that the nation shall be consulted, and that one House without the\nL252@P8: support of the nation shall not be allowed to domineer over the other…\nL253@P8: But when once we have come to the conclusion from all the circumstances of the case\nL254@P8: that the House of Commons is at one with the nation, it appears to me that – save in\nL255@P8: some very exceptional cases, save in the highest case of morality – in those cases in\nL256@P8: which a man would not set his hand to a certain proposition, though a revolution\nL257@P8: should follow from his refusal – it appears to me that the vocation of this House has\nL258@P8: passed away, that it must devolve the responsibility upon the nation, and may fairly\nL259@P8: accept the conclusion at which the nation has arrived.\nL260@P8: (Parliamentary Debates, Third Series, 17th June 1869, vol. 197, cols. 83–84)\nL261@P8: An amendment to delay giving the Irish Church Bill a second reading was defeated on 18th\nL262@P8-9: June 1869 by 179 votes to 146. 6\nL263@P9: Weston assesses the implications of Salisbury’s speech and the decision of the Lords to give\nL264@P9: the Bill a second reading as follows:\nL265@P9: Thanks to Salisbury’s leadership the House of Lords, if it chose to act again on such a\nL266@P9: theory, would enjoy a large measure of credibility … no defeated House, in the wake\nL267@P9: of a political disaster, ever received a more effective ideological gloss than Salisbury\nL268@P9: imported in 1869. His argument for giving the Irish Church Bill a second reading was\nL269@P9: remembered as long as the referendal theory was associated with the House of Lords.\nL270@P9: (Corinne Comstock Weston, ‘Salisbury and the Lords, 1868–1895’, Peers, Politics\nL271@P9: and Power: The House of Lords, 1603–1911, eds. Clyve Jones and David Lewis\nL272@P9: Jones (1986), p. 474)\nL273@P9: (ii) The Municipal Elections Bill 1872\nL274@P9: By 1872 the Liberal Government had suffered a number of by-election defeats,\nL275@P9: disappointment had been expressed by some of its supporters at compromises in the\nL276@P9: Education Act 1870, and a vigorous campaign had been conducted against its proposed\nL277@P9: licensing legislation, which was eventually passed in 1872. The Parliamentary and Municipal\nL278@P9: Elections Bill, which amended procedures at elections and introduced the secret ballot, was\nL279@P9: introduced in February 1872 and passed the Commons on 30th May. The views expressed by\nL280@P9: Lord Salisbury during the passage of the Irish Church Bill hardened into a definite principle\nL281@P9: when he urged the rejection of the Bill by the House of Lords. Writing to Lord Carnarvon on\nL282@P9: 20th February 1872 he outlined his strategy for dealing with the Bill:\nL283@P9: I am strongly for rejecting the Bill on the second reading, for this reason. It appears\nL284@P9: to me of vital necessity that our acceptance of Bills to which we are opposed should\nL285@P9: be regulated on some principle. If we listen to the Liberals we should accept all\nL286@P9: important Bills which had passed the House of Commons by a large majority. But\nL287@P9: that in effect would be to efface the House of Lords. Another principle, – which is, so\nL288@P9: far as I can gather, what commends itself to Derby – is to watch newspapers, public\nL289@P9: meetings and so forth, and only to reject when ‘public opinion’, thus ascertained,\nL290@P9: growls very loud. This plan gives a premium to bluster and will bring the House into\nL291@P9: contempt. The plan which I prefer is frankly to acknowledge that the nation is our\nL292@P9: Master, though the House of Commons is not, and to yield our own opinion only\nL293@P9: when the judgement of the nation has been challenged at the polls and decidedly\nL294@P9: expressed. This doctrine, it seems to me, has the advantage of being: (1)\nL295@P9: Theoretically sound. (2) Popular. (3) Safe against agitation, and (4) so rarely\nL296@P9: applicable as practically to place little fetter upon our independence. It is therefore\nL297@P9: rather as part of a general principle than because I attach a supreme importance to the\nL298@P9: question of the Ballot, that I am most earnestly desirous of inducing [The Duke of]\nL299@P9: Richmond [Leader of the Conservatives in the House of Lords] to resist the second\nL300@P9: reading.\nL301@P9: (Lady Gwendolen Cecil, Life of Robert Marquis of Salisbury (1921), vol. II,\nL302@P9-10: pp. 25–6) 7\nL303@P10: Salisbury’s speech during the debate on the motion to give the Bill a second reading on 10th\nL304@P10: June 1872 was significant for its insistence that the House of Lords, in deciding on a given\nL305@P10: measure, should look directly to the results of a General Election if its members were to learn\nL306@P10: the nation’s wishes; and in its singling out of the electorate as the source of ultimate political\nL307@P10: authority.\nL308@P10: I draw the widest possible distinction between the opinions of the House of Commons\nL309@P10: and the opinions of the Nation. Several of the speakers this evening have admitted\nL310@P10: what is the fact – namely, that neither this House nor any authority in this State or any\nL311@P10: other State can resist the deliberate and well–settled opinion of the Nation over which\nL312@P10: it rules. But that the House of Commons is the expression of the opinion of the\nL313@P10: Nation is a constitutional fiction which it is convenient for practical purposes to\nL314@P10: respect; but which is only literally true on certain occasions and on certain subjects. It\nL315@P10: is true literally when a question on which the House of Commons has been elected is\nL316@P10: under discussion – then it is undoubtedly true that the House of Commons represents\nL317@P10: the opinion of the Nation:– but when four years have gone by, and the memory of all\nL318@P10: the questions on which the House of Commons was elected has passed away, and\nL319@P10: when an obvious change has taken place in many opinions of the Government which\nL320@P10: the House of Commons was elected to support, the House of Commons represents\nL321@P10: only theoretically and not literally the opinions of the Nation …\nL322@P10: … the Members of the Government are not the same as they were when they went to\nL323@P10: the country – they were then mainly non-Ballot politicians … Therefore, I maintain,\nL324@P10: the country has never had a fair opportunity of considering whether it likes the Ballot\nL325@P10: or not … So far from admitting that because its concerns elections to the House of\nL326@P10: Commons we ought to obey the behests of that House, I should say, on the contrary, it\nL327@P10: is rather our duty to regard ourselves in this matter as agents of the Nation, and to see\nL328@P10: that the House of Commons, in thus tampering with the laws under which it was\nL329@P10: elected, has not transgressed the mandate it received.\nL330@P10: (Parliamentary Debates, Third Series, vol. 211, cols. 1493–1495)\nL331@P10: The Municipal Elections Bill was, however, given a second reading by the House of Lords by\nL332@P10: 86 votes to 56 on 10th June 1872.\nL333@P10: In the General Election of February 1874 the Conservatives under Disraeli won a majority in\nL334@P10: the House of Commons and held office until 1880.\nL335@P10: (iii) The development of the doctrine\nL336@P10: At the General Election in 1880 the Conservatives were defeated and the Liberals returned to\nL337@P10: power with a majority in the House of Commons over the Conservatives of 137. In 1881\nL338@P10: Lord Salisbury became Leader of the Opposition in the House of Lords and leader of the\nL339@P10-11: Conservative Party (a post shared until 1885 with Sir Stafford Northcote). 8\nL340@P11: In opposition, Salisbury elaborated the theory of the mandate. For example, in November\nL341@P11: 1880, at a speech to the Hackney Conservative Club, he argued that the duty of the House of\nL342@P11: Lords was to represent the enduring feelings of the nation:\nL343@P11: There are persons among us who maintain that it is the duty of the House of Lords to\nL344@P11: act as the pale shadow of the House of Commons, and to repeat in a feeble echo all\nL345@P11: that that House may decide to pass. I believe that, under any circumstances, such a\nL346@P11: doctrine cannot be sustained. Even if the alternative were, in a day which is far from\nL347@P11: us, the abolition of the House of Lords, I should still say it was better for the House of\nL348@P11: Lords to speak out its independent mind before the community, than to deceive it with\nL349@P11: the false pretences of a security that does not exist. Do not understand me to be\nL350@P11: maintaining that it is the function of the House of Lords to maintain opinions different\nL351@P11: from the mature convictions of the nation at large. Such is the constitution of our\n"
